 from that same PWM net. The base node Net-(Q2-B) has no other connection. The transistor therefore feeds back on the signal it is supposed to drive, rather than being driven from a separate source.\", \"expected\": \"A transistor stage should take its base drive from a separate control source, with its collector driving the load node, not its own controlled node.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"7\\\") (name \\\"Net-(Q2-B)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\")\\n      (node (ref \\\"Q2\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pinfunction \\\"B\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\\n      (node (ref \\\"R2\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\")))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"Q2\\\") (pin \\\"3\\\") (pinfunction \\\"C\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R2 PWM Net-_Q2-B_ 1k\"}], \"impact\": \"Q2 can pull the PWM net low whenever its base is driven through R2 from that net. This conflicts with the MCU output and with the gate pull-up on Q1, so the fan drive stage may not work as intended.\", \"confidence\": 0.25}], \"summary\": \"The netlist shows two MCUs (U2 ATtiny45 and U3 ATtiny13V) wired in parallel on every signal net, which is a likely bus-contention fault. A less certain concern is that Q2's base-drive resistor is fed from the same PWM net that Q2's collector controls. The spec is a generic repository overview and does not describe this board's circuit, so these findings rest on electrical reasoning alone.\"}",
